\DOC LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal across an inlined 4-word modular squaring
subroutine, swapping the current machine-state assumptions for those of the
successor state and adding the bound and congruence facts that characterise the
squared result.

\DESCRIBE
The tactic takes two arguments: an {int} and a {string list}. The integer is a
fixed numeric parameter (the same value throughout the observed traces). The
string list names the output and input quantities of the squaring step —
destination variable first, then source variable — and these determine the new
result variable and the term it is equated to in the added facts. The
conclusion retains its {eventually} postcondition but is restated about a fresh
later state variable. The assumptions describing the old state (program
counter, loaded bytes, register and memory reads, and the accumulated
{MAYCHANGE} relation) are discharged and replaced by the corresponding
assumptions about the new state; the {MAYCHANGE} frame may be extended by the
newly written memory component, and the memory assumption for the destination
location now reads a fresh variable. Two new arithmetic assumptions about that
fresh variable are added: a bound of the form {t < u} and a modular congruence
relating it to the square of the source quantity. Purely arithmetic assumptions
that do not mention the state are kept unchanged.
{
   A_arith, A_state(x1 ... xn) ?- eventually ... (old state)
   =============================================================  LOCAL_SQR_4_TAC n ["dest"; "src"]
   A_arith, A_state'(z1 ... zp), t < u, (congruence: fresh var vs. square of src)
     ?- eventually ... (new state)
}

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces only this single kind of step occurs, always on goals of
this symbolic-execution shape and always with the same integer argument.

\ENDDOC
